**Requirements to clarify.** Bounded (backpressure) or unbounded? Blocking `push`/`pop` or `try_` variants? Multiple producers and consumers? Fairness? Need to close or shut down? Element type (copyable or move-only)?

**API.** `push(T)`, `T pop()` (blocks when empty), `bool try_pop(T&)`, optional `pop_for(timeout)`, `close()`, `size()`.

**Design (bounded, MPMC, mutex plus two condition variables).**

```cpp
template <class T> class BlockingQueue {
    std::queue<T> q_; std::mutex m_;
    std::condition_variable not_empty_, not_full_;
    size_t cap_; bool closed_ = false;
public:
    explicit BlockingQueue(size_t cap) : cap_(cap) {}
    bool push(T v) {
        std::unique_lock lk(m_);
        not_full_.wait(lk, [&]{ return q_.size() < cap_ || closed_; });
        if (closed_) return false;
        q_.push(std::move(v));
        not_empty_.notify_one();
        return true;
    }
    std::optional<T> pop() {
        std::unique_lock lk(m_);
        not_empty_.wait(lk, [&]{ return !q_.empty() || closed_; });
        if (q_.empty()) return std::nullopt;   // closed and drained
        T v = std::move(q_.front()); q_.pop();
        not_full_.notify_one();
        return v;
    }
    void close() { { std::lock_guard lk(m_); closed_ = true; }
                   not_empty_.notify_all(); not_full_.notify_all(); }
};
```

**Key points.** Always wait with a predicate (spurious wakeups); modify state under the lock; `close()` wakes everyone so consumers can exit; return values by move to avoid copying under the lock.

**Trade-offs and scaling.** One mutex serialises everything; fine for modest rates. Improvements: separate head and tail locks with a dummy node (Michael-Scott two-lock queue), batching, a ring buffer with atomics for single-producer single-consumer, or a lock-free MPMC queue (needs care with memory reclamation and the ABA problem). Test with many threads, ThreadSanitizer and stress tests, and check exception safety.
